      en: "Wadi al-Rummah is the longest wadi (dry valley) in Saudi Arabia, stretching approximately 600 kilometers from the Hijaz Mountains to the Al-Sar basin. Once a perennial river flowing to the Persian Gulf, it now carries water only after heavy rains. The wadi is a key agricultural region, relying on groundwater for irrigation. Major cities like Buraydah and Unaizah lie along its course. Date palms and wheat fields dominate the landscape. Historically, the wadi served as a trade route connecting the interior to the coast.",
